{{Culture of Balisca}}
A '''guaso''' ({{lang-bl|ˈwaso}}; [[Maroniko language|Maroniko]] and [[Mozarabic]]: ''yaso'') is a [[Balisca|Baliscan]] countryman and skilled horseman, similar to the [[United States|American]] {{W|cowboy}}, the [[Mexico|Mexican]] {{W|charro}} (and its northern equivalent, the {{W|vaquero}}), the {{W|gaucho}} of the [[W:Rio de la Plata|Rio de la Plata]] and {{W|Rio Grande Do Sul}}, and the [[W:Australia|Australian]] [[W:Stockman (Australia)|stockman]]. The guasos are an important part of [[Culture of Balisca|Araucarian folkloric culture]] and are a vital part of parades, festivals, holidays, and popular music. A female guaso is called a '''''guasa'''''. Guasos are found all over [[Araucaria]] and [[Cerdanya|Northern Cerdanya]], and the rural interiors of [[Bayara]] and [[Estuaria]]. The major difference between the ''guaso'' and the ''gaucho'' is that the guasos are typically involved in farming as well as cattle herding.

They ride [[Baliscan Horse|horses]] and typically wear a straw or leather hats. They also wear a {{W|poncho}}—called a ''manta'' or a ''{{W|chamanto}}'' (although this was originally reserved to land owners, as it is much more expensive)—over a short Andalusian waist jacket, as well as tooled leather legging over boots with rawhide leather spur holders that sustain a long-shanked spur with 4-inch rowels, and many other typical garments. 

